      "prompt": "add the following feedback to the handoff as well: \"Feedback grounded in exactly what happened this session — split into what the tooling caused and what I caused, since both are fixable.\n1. Tool descriptions don't match user vocabulary. My first search for \"open memory vault for repository\" returned zero tools. mnemos_wake's description leads with mechanics (\"Session boot, budget-capped. Two steps…\") and jargon (Q-value, PPR, stage meta-learning). It never contains the phrase a user actually says: \"open the vault.\" Descriptions are retrieval prompts — lead with intent (\"Call this first to open/load memory for a session\"), include common phrasings as trigger text, push mechanics to the end. This one change would have collapsed turn 1 to a single call.\n2. No repo→project bridge. You said \"for this repository.\" The vault knew the project as roust; the disk says Alarm App. I bridged that by coincidence (note titles + grep of the repo). The fix: store project↔path aliases in the vault and let mnemos_wake accept path= (or resolve cwd), falling back to mnemos_suggest_scope automatically. Silent resolution, zero interaction.\n3. The ask-first gate forces an interaction by design. Wake step 1 returned project_choice_required instructing me to ask you which project — the exact friction you're trying to eliminate. Make the gate resolve-first: resolve silently when the user names it, the path matches, or suggest_scope confidence is high; only surface the question on true ambiguity. The question UI itself is good — it's the default path that's wrong.\n4. Step 1 of the two-step wake was redundant here. The step-2 response contained everything step 1 gave (identity line, roster, policy) plus the project block. If the caller passes a confident project, one call should suffice; the two-phase contract should only be mandatory when the project is unknown.\n5. My miss on \"wake\". When you said run wake project=roust, I went looking for a workflow file instead of mapping \"wake\" → mnemos_wake, which I'd already discovered. Partly on me — but stronger intent-first naming/descriptions (e.g. mentioning it's the session-start verb) defends against that class of error too.\nWhat already worked: memory_sql answered the roster question in one query, wake's structured JSON (projectWake.currentState, warm notes) parsed cleanly, and policy (soft=allow) ran with no approval prompts. Keep those.\nNet effect if you ship 1–3: \"open the vault\" becomes one tool call, no round trips, no questions — invisible.\"",
